  "requirement": "For every `{impl}`, `{cite}`, and `{test}` reference in a project source file, the evidence file for the referenced requirement or example MUST contain an input whose resolved source ranges cover the reference's file and line. Habeas MUST report every uncovered reference with its role, full name, source file, and line number.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict: the requirement is met.** The code implements it as stated, and the tests cover the main cases. I judged this from the supplied excerpts only. I did not read the rest of `src/evidence.rs` or run the tests.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `validate_references` in `src/evidence.rs` (the code cited at roughly lines 920–984) calls `collect_references` to scan the repository's text files.\n  - The scan skips only root-level `.git`, `target`, `book` and `evidence`.\n  - It takes the line number from the byte offset of each match.\n  - It extracts `{impl}`, `{cite}` and `{test}` roles via `reference_roles` in `src/lib.rs`, which skips Markdown literals (inline code and fenced blocks).\n- Each non-term reference is grouped under its target requirement or example.\n- For each target, `evidence/<name>.json` is loaded through `evidence_input_ranges`.\n  - That function resolves every input with `InputSelector::resolve`. It covers file/line, `rust_file`/`rust_item` and requirements-glob inputs.\n  - It returns canonicalized file paths with `line_start`/`line_end`, which is the `SourceRange` shape in `src/inputs.rs`.\n- A reference counts as covered only if some input has the same file and `line_start <= line <= line_end`.\n- Otherwise the code pushes `\"<evidence file>: no input covers {<role>}`<full name>` at <file>:<line>\"`. That reports the role, full name, source file and line, as required.\n- A missing or unparsable evidence file gives an empty range list, so all of its references are reported as uncovered. That is the safe behavior.\n- References to unknown targets are reported separately.\n\n**Tests**\n- `source_references_are_covered_by_evidence_inputs` is the positive case. `{impl}` and `{cite}` in `src/main.rs` and `{test}` in `md/notes.md` are each covered by file-range inputs.\n  - Inline-code and fenced `{cite}` references to a missing name are correctly ignored. This confirms the references that count are real ones.\n- `reports_unknown_references_and_uncovered_lines` is the negative case. Two `{test}` references sit on lines 1 and 2, and the evidence covers only line 1.\n  - It asserts the error `no input covers {test}`foo.bar.ready` at src/main.rs:2`, which includes role, name, file and line.\n  - It asserts exactly two errors, so the covered line 1 is not reported.\n- `cited_example_with_a_test_and_matching_evidence_validates` runs end to end with Rust-item and requirements-glob inputs. It passes with full coverage.\n  - It then empties the example's inputs and asserts `no input covers` and `src/main.rs:1`.\n\n**Gaps:** none are acknowledged and none material. Symlink and canonicalization edge cases are handled by canonicalizing both sides, though not specifically tested.",
